<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.2.3: Radicals and Rational Exponents</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01251</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.2.3: Radicals and Rational Exponents</md:title>
    <md:uuid>384cfeec-ed31-4dda-abd7-154c799c1aed</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity (20 minutes)</title>
<para>This activity allows students to write radicals as terms with rational exponents and vice versa.</para>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>Arrange students into groups of 2.</para>

<title> Student Activity</title>
<para>If \(\sqrt[n]{a^m}\) is a real number and \(n\;\geq\;2\), then \(\sqrt[n]{a^m}\;=\;a^\frac mn\).</para>
<para>So radicals can be rewritten as rational exponents.</para>
<para>With a partner, write each radical as a term with rational exponents on your own paper. Then, turn and talk to your partner. Check your answers when you are ready.
</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(\sqrt[8]x\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\(x^\frac18\) </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(\sqrt[3]{x^2}\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\(x^\frac23\) </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(\sqrt[11]{x^5}\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\(x^\frac5{11}\) 
  
Now, with a partner, rewrite each term as an equivalent radical. Check your answer to each.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(x^\frac12\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\(\sqrt[2]x\;\) or \(\;\sqrt x\) </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(x^\frac17\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\(\sqrt[7]x\;\) </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(x^\frac45\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\(\sqrt[5]{x^4}\;\) </para>

<title>Video: Learning About Rational Exponents</title>
<para>Watch the following video to learn more about rational exponents.</para>
<media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text"><iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/184e8762df04ba9f7374004a7694c2581541f978"/></media><title>Anticipated Misconceptions</title>
<para>When writing radicals as terms with rational exponents, it is important that the rational exponent has the original power on the top of the fraction and the index/root on the bottom.</para>
<para>A graphic organizer can help students as a reference for this.</para>
<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>Invite pairs to share their answers. Allow students to make their own examples and then trade with other partner groups if time allows.</para>
<list>
  <item> First, have each pair write an example as a radical and switch with another pair. Then each pair writes the term with the rational exponent that matches their radical. </item>
  <item> Have each pair write a term with a rational exponent and then switch with another pair. Each pair writes the term as a radical. </item>
</list>
<title>5.2.3: Self Check</title>
<para>After the activity, students will answer the following question to check their understanding of the concepts explored in the activity.</para>
<para>QUESTION:</para>
<para>Rewrite \(\sqrt[10]{x^7}\) as a term with a rational exponent.</para>
<table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>Answers</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>Feedback</emphasis></entry></row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry> \(x^\frac{10}7\) </entry>
      <entry> Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The 7 is the exponent, and the 10 is the index. The answer is \(x^\frac{7}{10}\). </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry> \(x^\frac{7}{10}\) </entry>
      <entry> That’s correct! Check yourself: The exponent gets written over the root. </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry> \(10x^7\) </entry>
      <entry> Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The 10 is the root, not a coefficient. The answer is \(x^\frac{7}{10}\). </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry> \(\frac7{10}x\) </entry>
      <entry> Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The fraction should be the exponent. The answer is \(x^\frac{7}{10}\). </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<title>5.2.3: Additional Resources</title>
<para>The following content is available to students who would like more support based on their experience with the self check. Students will not automatically have access to this content, so you may wish to share it with those who could benefit from it.</para>
<title>Rewriting Radicals</title>
<para>Example 1</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/4c149ceb45a3e32a53d2f947157509651b7c3495"/></media></figure></para>
<para>When writing radicals as a term with a rational exponent, the exponent becomes the numerator of the fraction in the exponent, the root or index.</para>
<para>In the example above, the 2 is the exponent and becomes the top of the fraction. The 5 is the index and becomes the bottom of the fraction.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Identify the index.
  5</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis>Identify the exponent (inside the radical).
  2</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Write the power over the root.
  \(8^{ \frac25}\)</para>
<newline/><para>Let’s simplify a rational exponent with a variable. </para>
<para>Example 2</para>
<para>\(\sqrt[4]{5x^3}\)</para>
<para dir="ltr"><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Identify the index.
  4</para>

<para dir="ltr"><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Identify the exponent (inside the radical).
  3</para>
<emphasis>
</emphasis>
<para dir="ltr"><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Write the power over the root.
  \(5x^{\frac34}\)</para>


<title>Try It: Rewriting Radicals</title>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Write \(\sqrt[3]{12^2}\) as a term with a rational exponent.</item>
</list>
<para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<title>Solution</title>
<para>Here is how to rewrite the radical expression:</para>

<para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>Identify the index.
  3</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Identify the exponent (inside the radical).
  2</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Write the power over the root.
  \(12^{\frac23}\)</para>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Write \(\sqrt[6]{x^5}\) as a term with a rational exponent.</item>
</list>
<para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
<title>Solution</title>
<para>Here is how to rewrite the radical expression:</para>

<para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Identify the index.
  6</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Identify the exponent (inside the radical).
  5</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3-</emphasis> Write the power over the root.
  \(x^\frac56\)</para>

</section>
